parent\" sizes=\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\" srcSet=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_6494.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>108 Parshwanath, No. 11 – Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: The image of Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath is black in colour, seated in the padmasana posture with a beautiful parikar (surrounding ornamental frame), 8 inches tall and 5 inches wide. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 5 serpent hoods.</p><p>Location: This temple is situated in Kharwado, in the city of Khambhat, Gujarat.</p><p>History: The ancient name of Khambhat was &quot;Trambavati,&quot; and today it is known in English as &quot;Cambay.&quot; Since ancient times Khambhat has been very famous for its Jain temples.</p><p>The image of Lord Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath is made of sapphire (neelam). This image, too, has its own magnificent history. It was consecrated by the shravaka (lay devotee) Ashadhi of the previous cycle of twenty-four Tirthankaras (gayi chovisi). Thereafter it was worshipped by various deities for thousands of years, being venerated at different places over time; it was last worshipped by King Krishna. At the time of the destruction of Dwarka, a merchant (shreshthi) consigned it to the sea. A shreshthi of Krantipur later recovered this image from the sea with a sign and assistance from a goddess. Along with it, two other images were recovered — one of Lord Shri Parshwanath and one of Lord Shri Neminath — which even today stand as the image of Shri Parshwanath at Charup and the image of Shri Neminath in the village of Patan.</p><p>From the era of the twentieth Tirthankara to the era of the last Tirthankara, many miraculous events took place here. After that, the image remained lost for years. In Vikram Samvat 1119, the commentator on the nine Angas (Navangi Tikakar), Shri Abhayadeva Suriji, receiving divine inspiration, composed the Jayatihuana Stotra with great devotion on the bank of the Sedhi river; this pleased the presiding deity (adhishthayak dev), and this supremely miraculous image reappeared from beneath the earth at that very spot, before the eyes of numerous devotees. It was through bathing in the water used to bathe this image (nhavan jal) that Shri Abhayadeva Suriji&#x27;s body was cured of disease. The omniscient scholar of the Kali era (Kalikal Sarvagya), Shri Hemachandracharya, took initiation (diksha) here in Vikram Samvat 1150 and began his education.</p><p>Other Temples: 1. Shri Shantinath–Neminath Temple. 2. Shri Shantinath Temple, which contains an ancient and attractive image of Parshwanath inscribed with the year V.S. 1383; the other images in this temple date to the 16th century. 3. Shri Mallinath Swami Temple. 4. Shri Chandraprabhu Swami Temple. Khambhat has more than 73 temples. The temples of Khambhat are the pride of Gujarat.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: This image is very ancient.</p><p>Directions: Khambhat is 80 km from Vadodara, 28 km from Manilaxmi Tirtha, and 90 km from Ahmedabad. Khambhat has several dharamshalas (pilgrim rest-houses) and bhojanshalas (dining halls), several upashrayas (monastic lodgings), pathshalas (schools), and jnanabhandars (manuscript libraries). Many ancient manuscripts and images are preserved in these jnanabhandars.</p><p>Scripture References: Many scriptures, verses, and ras (narrative poems) were composed here by numerous great acharyas, monks, and others. In V.S. 1422, Shri Vinayaprabhaji wrote the &quot;Shri Gautam Swami Ras&quot; in Khambhat. In the mid-16th century, Shri Nansurji wrote &quot;Vichar Sogath,&quot; &quot;Gajsukumar Rajarshi Sajjhaya,&quot; and &quot;Panch Tirth Stavan.&quot; Shri Kushalabhji wrote the &quot;Shri Stambhan Parshwanath Stavan&quot; in V.S. 1653. In the 17th century, Shri Rishabhdasji wrote several books in Gujarati. During his chaturmas (four-month monsoon retreat) here in V.S. 1727, Shri Labhvijayji wrote &quot;Shripal Mayasundari Ras.&quot; Shri Matisagarji wrote &quot;Khambhat ni Tirumala&quot; and &quot;Dhanna Rishi Chaupai.&quot; Shri Atmaramji Maharaj wrote &quot;Agyan Timir Bhaskar&quot; in V.S. 1942.</p><p>Hymn of Praise: By the teaching of Lord Nemi in the bygone cycle of twenty-four Tirthankaras, the shravaka Ashadhi worshipped this sapphire image of Lord Parshwa; Nagarjuna and the eighth Baladeva too worshipped him — &quot;With loving devotion I bow in reverence to Shri Sthambhan Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hreem Shri Sthambhan Parshwanathaya Namah&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Kharwado, Post: Khambhat, District: Kheda, Gujarat–388620. Phone: 02698-220221.</p><p>The Sixth Life: King Vajranabh and Kurangak the Bhil</p><p>After completing his lifespan in the twelfth Devalok (heavenly abode), Marubhuti&#x27;s soul was born as the son of King Vajravirya in the city of Shubhankara, in the Sugandhi Vijay region of western Mahavideh. He was named Vajranabh. In his youth he married a princess named Vijaya. Years later a son was born to him, named Chakrayudh.</p><p>Once the Tirthankara Lord Kshemankar came travelling to that region, and King Vajranabh went to the samavasarana (divine preaching assembly) to hear his sermon. On hearing the Lord&#x27;s sweet words, a feeling of renunciation (vairagya) awakened in Vajranabh&#x27;s heart. Returning to the palace, he explained his intention to his son and others and had Chakrayudh crowned king. Then, with a renunciation born of true understanding, Vajranabh accepted monastic vows before the Tirthankara Lord Kshemankar. Devoted to spiritual practice in order to gain special knowledge from the Lord, the monk Vajranabh became a scholar well versed in scripture (gitartha jnani). The Lord granted him leave to wander independently. By the power of a sky-faring occult art (akashagamini vidya), he travelled to the Sukachchha Vijay region and began to wander among its mountains.</p><p>Kamath&#x27;s soul, after completing its span in hell and passing through several brief lives in animal form, was born as a Bhil (forest-dwelling tribesman) named Kurangak in Sukachchha Vijay. Owing to the rise of sinful karma he was born into an inauspicious lineage and so received no good upbringing from birth; nor, even as he grew older, did he find any occasion for good influence, and so his bad tendencies only increased. His mind became absorbed in vices such as wandering the forest, hunting, and eating meat.</p><p>One day the Bhil Kurangak saw the monk Vajranabh in the forest. Because of the enmity carried over from their earlier birth, the fire of hatred blazed up in his eyes the instant he saw the monk. He strung an arrow to his bow, took aim at the monk&#x27;s chest, and shot. As the arrow struck, a fountain of blood burst from his chest. Chanting &quot;Namo Arihantanam,&quot; absorbed in meditation upon the Supreme Lord, he fell to the ground in a state of perfect equanimity (samata bhava). Monk Vajranabh had been born in Sugandhi Vijay and had also taken initiation there, but his karma led him to practise austerities in Sukachchha Vijay and brought about this encounter with the Bhil. It is for this that it is said, &quot;Oh karma! Strange indeed are your ways...&quot;</p><p>This same prince of Kashi, this same god of Mount Sammed Shikhar,</p><p>This same sun of Ahikshetra, ever awakened, who fills the heart with pure light!</p><p>This is the very Parshwa whom devotees dug out of the earth at Khambhat —</p><p>Chant the rosary of Parshwa, the wish-fulfilling gem, and every worry and anxiety shall vanish!</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-013\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Sanvara Parshwanath (Shri Shamala Parshwanath)</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-rig