"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"95de4eda-e7de-478b-a472-31f1f0ce7798\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_6601.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath फोटो 4\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"25cb8061-2bcd-4c31-a1c6-7b61cf44ed52\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_6607.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath फोटो 5\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"ee0ac9a9-8f03-4777-97af-b9984f7566e7\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_6494.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath फोटो 6\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"108 Parshwanath, No. 11 – Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: The image of Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath is black in colour, seated in the padmasana posture with a beautiful parikar (surrounding ornamental frame), 8 inches tall and 5 inches wide. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 5 serpent hoods.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This temple is situated in Kharwado, in the city of Khambhat, Gujarat.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"History: The ancient name of Khambhat was \\\\\\\"Trambavati,\\\\\\\" and today it is known in English as \\\\\\\"Cambay.\\\\\\\" Since ancient times Khambhat has been very famous for its Jain temples.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"The image of Lord Shri Sthambhan Parshwanath is made of sapphire (neelam). This image, too, has its own magnificent history. It was consecrated by the shravaka (lay devotee) Ashadhi of the previous cycle of twenty-four Tirthankaras (gayi chovisi). Thereafter it was worshipped by various deities for thousands of years, being venerated at different places over time; it was last worshipped by King Krishna. At the time of the destruction of Dwarka, a merchant (shreshthi) consigned it to the sea. A shreshthi of Krantipur later recovered this image from the sea with a sign and assistance from a goddess. Along with it, two other images were recovered — one of Lord Shri Parshwanath and one of Lord Shri Neminath — which even today stand as the image of Shri Parshwanath at Charup and the image of Shri Neminath in the village of Patan.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"From the era of the twentieth Tirthankara to the era of the last Tirthankara, many miraculous events took place here. After that, the image remained lost for years. In Vikram Samvat 1119, the commentator on the nine Angas (Navangi Tikakar), Shri Abhayadeva Suriji, receiving divine inspiration, composed the Jayatihuana Stotra with great devotion on the bank of the Sedhi river; this pleased the presiding deity (adhishthayak dev), and this supremely miraculous image reappeared from beneath the earth at that very spot, before the eyes of numerous devotees. It was through bathing in the water used to bathe this image (nhavan jal) that Shri Abhayadeva Suriji's body was cured of disease. The omniscient scholar of the Kali era (Kalikal Sarvagya), Shri Hemachandracharya, took initiation (diksha) here in Vikram Samvat 1150 and began his education.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: 1. Shri Shantinath–Neminath Temple. 2. Shri Shantinath Temple, which contains an ancient and attractive image of Parshwanath inscribed with the year V.S. 1383; the other images in this temple date to the 16th century. 3. Shri Mallinath Swami Temple. 4. Shri Chandraprabhu Swami Temple. Khambhat has more than 73 temples. The temples of Khambhat are the pride of Gujarat.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Art and Sculpture: This image is very ancient.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"8\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: Khambhat is 80 km from Vadodara, 28 km from Manilaxmi Tirtha, and 90 km from Ahmedabad. Khambhat has several dharamshalas (pilgrim rest-houses) and bhojanshalas (dining halls), several upashrayas (monastic lodgings), pathshalas (schools), and jnanabhandars (manuscript libraries). Many ancient manuscripts and images are preserved in these jnanabhandars.\\\"}],\\\"$L23\\\",\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\",\\\"$L28\\\",\\\"$L29\\\",\\\"$L2a\\\",\\\"$L2b\\\",\\\"$L2c\\\",\\\"$L2d\\\",\\\"$L2e\\\",\\\"$L2f\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-013\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\